 # Regularization and Geometry Constraints
 
-Also called: Regularization and Artifact Reduction, Geometric Priors in 3DGS
+Also called: Geometric Regularization, Depth Priors, Frequency Constraints
 
 ## Overview
-Regularization and Geometry Constraints address the inherent ambiguity of single-view or sparse-view reconstruction in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS), where color-only supervision often leads to incorrect Gaussian positioning, floating artifacts, or collapsed geometry [S1, S3]. This family of methods introduces auxiliary loss terms or structural constraints to enforce geometric plausibility, spectral consistency, or physical priors, distinguishing itself from sibling methods like [Base 3DGS Optimization](7549-base-3dgs-optimization.md) which relies solely on photometric reconstruction loss. Unlike [Physical Parameter Optimization](4111-physical-parameter-optimization.md) which optimizes physical properties, these constraints directly guide the optimization trajectory of centers, opacities, and shapes to prevent overfitting and improve structural fidelity [S1, S3].
+Regularization and Geometry Constraints are techniques applied during the differentiable training of 3D Gaussian Splatting to enforce geometric plausibility, prevent overfitting, and stabilize optimization. They solve the problem where photometric loss alone leads to floating artifacts, blurry surfaces, or unstable camera poses by adding auxiliary constraints on depth, frequency content, or Gaussian shape. This family sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Physical Parameter Optimization](4111-physical-parameter-optimization.md) or [WildGaussians](6064-wildgaussians.md) by focusing explicitly on the structural integrity of the scene representation (geometry and high-frequency detail) rather than appearance, camera models, or training efficiency.
 
